As a [[Premier League]] team, Chelsea entered the competition in the Third Round, where they were drawn at home to [[Portsmouth F.C.|Portsmouth]]. After a goalless first half [[Juan Mata]] struck early in the second, before [[Ramires]] hit two in two minutes and [[Frank Lampard]] scored in stoppage time to give Chelsea a 4–0 win.<ref>{{cite news |title=Chelsea 4–0 Portsmouth |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/16376766 |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=8 January 2012 |access-date=15 April 2012 }}</ref> Chelsea then travelled to face [[West London derby|local rivals]] [[Queens Park Rangers F.C.|Queens Park Rangers]] at [[Loftus Road]] in the Fourth Round, where a controversial Mata penalty was enough to see them through.<ref>{{cite news |first=Nabil |last=Hassan |title=QPR 0–1 Chelsea |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/16664272.stm |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=28 January 2012 |access-date=15 April 2012 }}</ref>

[[File:TorresyMeirelesBlues.jpg|thumb|left|[[Raul Meireles]] (left) and [[Fernando Torres]] (right) during Chelsea's Sixth Round match against [[Leicester City F.C.|Leicester City]]]]
Chelsea were drawn to play at home against [[Birmingham City F.C.|Birmingham City]] in the Fifth Round, with Birmingham dominating the first half through a [[David Murphy (footballer, born 1984)|David Murphy]] goal.<ref name="cfc_bcfc">{{cite news |first=Owen |last=Phillips |title=Chelsea 1–1 Birmingham |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/16994719 |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=18 February 2012 |access-date=15 April 2012 }}</ref> [[Daniel Sturridge]] scored in the second half to force a replay, which was played on 6 March at Birmingham, just two days after [[André Villas-Boas]] was relieved of his duties as Chelsea manager.<ref name="cfc_bcfc"/> Birmingham held Chelsea throughout the first half, and looked the stronger side at times, but after Mata and [[Raul Meireles]] scored twice in five minutes, Chelsea dominated the remainder of the game until the final whistle.<ref>{{cite news |first=Phil |last=McNulty |title=Birmingham 0–2 Chelsea |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/17188029 |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=6 March 2012 |access-date=15 April 2012 }}</ref> In the Sixth Round, Chelsea overcame [[Leicester City F.C.|Leicester City]] at home in a 5–2 thriller.<ref name="cfc_lcfc">{{cite news |first=Graham |last=Chase |title=Chelsea 5–2 Leicester |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/17330279 |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=18 March 2012 |access-date=15 April 2012 }}</ref> [[Gary Cahill]] scored his first FA Cup goal for Chelsea from a corner in the 12th minute, with [[Salomon Kalou]] following up five minutes later.<ref name="cfc_lcfc"/> In the second half, [[Fernando Torres]] ended a goal drought of over 25 hours to score, before [[Jermaine Beckford]] gave Leicester a late lifeline.<ref name="cfc_lcfc"/> Torres scored again in the 85th minute to put the result beyond doubt, although [[Ben Marshall (footballer)|Ben Marshall]] grabbed one back three minutes later, before Meireles scored in the 90th minute to send Chelsea through to the semi-final.<ref name="cfc_lcfc"/>

In the semi-final, Chelsea faced local rivals [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]] at Wembley. The first half was even, with both sides enjoying long spells of possession, until [[Didier Drogba]] put Chelsea ahead just before the break.<ref name="thfc_cfc">{{cite news |first=Phil |last=McNulty |title=Tottenham 1–5 Chelsea |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/17649443 |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=15 April 2012 |access-date=15 April 2012 }}</ref> Chelsea scored at the start of the second half. However, Juan Mata's goal that did not appear to cross the line, but referee [[Martin Atkinson]] awarded the goal.<ref name="thfc_cfc"/> [[Gareth Bale]] put Tottenham back in it with a goal shortly after a [[Petr Čech]] challenge on [[Emmanuel Adebayor]].<ref name="thfc_cfc"/> Despite the goal, Tottenham never took control of the game and [[Ramires]] scored in the 77th minute, followed minutes later by a [[Frank Lampard]] free kick, and a [[Florent Malouda]] strike in the fourth minute of added time. This secured Chelsea's place in their first FA Cup Final since their [[2010 FA Cup final|2010]] triumph over Portsmouth.

