==Post-match==
[[File:Dimatteo2.jpg|thumb|Interim Chelsea manager [[Roberto Di Matteo]] hoped the win would earn him the position on a permanent basis.]]
Chelsea's victory meant they won the FA Cup for the seventh time. Caretaker manager [[Roberto Di Matteo]] was relaxed about his prospects of securing the full-term following their victory: "The boss [ [[Roman Abramovich]] ] will make a decision at the end of the season. I'm very relaxed about it and the players will be fine, I am a very fortunate person so it is not an issue." Di Matteo was delighted with his players, as they survived Liverpool's attacks in the final minutes to secure victory: "I am very pleased for the players," said Di Matteo. "We have had a difficult season with a lot of criticism but they have responded to all the adversity we have faced. I'm very pleased and satisfied with our performance today." Chelsea captain [[John Terry]] was equally pleased with the performance, but turned his attention to the [[2012 UEFA Champions League Final]], where they would face [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]]: "It's fantastic, it's what we live for. We've still got a massive trophy, the Champions League - that's the target for the owner from day dot. We've done Robbie the world of good and it can do him no harm."<ref>{{cite news |first=Phil |last=McNulty |title=Roberto Di Matteo 'relaxed' over Chelsea job |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/17971923 |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=5 May 2012 |access-date=2 June 2015 }}</ref>

Liverpool manager [[Kenny Dalglish]] was adamant that his players would improve following the defeat: "They've been in two cup finals in this season – they'll have enjoyed the first one more than the second, the lads will benefit from the experience. You can't give a team like Chelsea a two-goal head start." When asked about his future as manager of the club Dalglish stated: "We'll assess the season at the end of the season."<ref name="liverpoolbbc">{{cite news |title=Kenny Dalglish hopes Liverpool learn from cup finals |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/17971918 |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=5 May 2012 |access-date=2 June 2015 }}</ref> Captain [[Steven Gerrard]] echoed Dalglish's sentiments: "With the players we have in that dressing room, we could have done better. But we are Liverpool. We will bounce back. We will strengthen in the summer and come back fighting again. That's what Liverpool Football Club does."<ref>{{cite news |first=Dominic |last=Fifield |title=Kenny Dalglish laments Liverpool's naivety against Chelsea |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2012/may/05/kenny-dalglish-liverpool-naivety-chelsea |work=The Guardian |publisher=Guardian News and Media |location=London |date=5 May 2012 |access-date=2 June 2015 }}</ref> Striker [[Andy Carroll]], who scored one goal and had another disallowed, thought the ball had crossed the line: "I thought it was over the line and you know better than me but I thought it was. I thought it hit the other side of the bar but I haven't seen it back."<ref name="liverpoolbbc"/>

The two sides met in the Premier League three days after the final, a match that Liverpool won 4–1.<ref>{{cite news |first=Phil |last=Dawkes |title=Liverpool 4–1 Chelsea |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/17904221 |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=8 May 2012 |access-date=2 June 2012 }}</ref> Liverpool finished the season in eighth place and Dalglish was subsequently sacked 8 days later on 16 May 2012 and replaced by [[Swansea City F.C.|Swansea City]] manager [[Brendan Rodgers]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Kenny Dalglish sacked as Liverpool manager |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/18073446 |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=16 May 2012 |access-date=2 June 2012 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first=Ben |last=Smith |title=Brendan Rodgers agrees deal to become new Liverpool manager |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/18235961 |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=30 May 2012 |access-date=2 June 2015 }}</ref> Chelsea would finish the season in sixth place; however, they won the [[2011–12 UEFA Champions League]], beating Bayern Munich 4–3 in a [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]] after the final finished 1–1 after extra time.<ref>{{cite news |first=Phil |last=McNulty |title=Bayern Munich 1–1 Chelsea |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/18044385 |publisher=BBC Sport (British Broadcasting Corporation) |date=19 May 2012 |access-date=2 June 2015 }}</ref> Chelsea's victory in the FA Cup final set up a [[FA Community Shield|Community Shield]] match against [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]], the winners of the [[2011–12 Premier League]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Bradley|first=Tom|date=18 May 2012|title=Villa Park to host 2012 FA Community Shield|url=https://www.goal.com/en-gb/news/2896/premier-league/2012/05/18/3111454/villa-park-to-host-2012-fa-community-shield|access-date=24 November 2020|publisher=[[Goal (website)|Goal.com]]}}</ref>

The match was broadcast live in the United Kingdom, in [[High-definition television|HD]] on [[ITV1]] and also on [[ESPN (UK)|ESPN]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Cup Final kick-off time |url=http://www.thefa.com/TheFACup/News/2012/Apr/fa-cup-final-kick-off-time |website=TheFA.com |publisher=The Football Association |date=16 April 2012 |access-date=6 June 2012 }}</ref> A peak of 11.2&nbsp;million saw the climax of the game on ITV1 with 8.9&nbsp;million viewers on average watching the live match coverage. It was the highest-rated final since the [[2007 FA Cup final|2007]] final. ESPN's coverage of the final attracted 397,000 viewers.<ref>{{cite news |title=Chelsea v Liverpool FA Cup final scores peak of 11 million viewers |url=https://www.theguardian.com/media/2012/may/08/chelsea-liverpool-fa-cup-11-million-viewers |work=The Guardian |publisher=Guardian News and Media |date=8 May 2012 |access-date=6 June 2012 }}</ref>

